<article class="reading">
  <h1>How I Think</h1>

  <p>Good design makes people feel like they're in control.</p>

  <p>I've been designing and building software for 25 years, and taking ideas from a sketch to production code. I've led design teams for years as a player coach.</p>

  <section class="how-i-think-section" aria-labelledby="design-heading">
    <h2 id="design-heading">My design process</h2>
    <p>AI does the volume and I do the judgment. Here's my current approach:</p>

    {% include "components/think-process.njk" %}

    <p>Most of my time goes to deciding what the problem is, deciding what's good, and getting it shipped. AI can speed up, but not replace these parts.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="how-i-think-section" aria-labelledby="beliefs-heading">
    <h2 id="beliefs-heading">What I believe</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Design is decision-making.</strong> The ability to clarify tradeoffs, prioritize, and rally a team around an idea.</li>
      <li><strong>Slow down to go fast.</strong> AI makes it easy to build something polished, but this risks moving <a href="https://www.seangood.win/articles/high-fidelity-low-definition">really fast in the wrong direction</a>. Unless the change is small, it's important getting the problem right first.</li>
      <li><strong>Rough and directionally correct over beautiful and wrong.</strong> A good prototype shortens debates and gets real feedback fast.</li>
      <li><strong>Evidence beats opinions.</strong> I'd rather watch one customer struggle with a flow than hear ten opinions about what they'd probably want.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="/blog/product-design-process/">Process is a tool, not a religion</a>.</strong> The team and the problem define the process, not the other way around.</li>
      <li><strong>Know which doors are one-way.</strong> Most decisions are cheap to undo. A few aren't, and those are worth slowing down for.</li>
    </ul>

    {% include "components/think-doors.njk" %}
  </section>

  <section class="how-i-think-section" aria-labelledby="trust-heading">
    <h2 id="trust-heading">Designing AI that people trust</h2>
    <p>What AI should automate? What shouldn't it? It depends on what a mistake costs.</p>

    <p>Forgetting the extra coffee on a Door Dash order? Annoying, but an inconvenience at best. A payroll mistake or error in legal paperwork? That could ruin someone's day.</p>

    {% include "components/think-spectrum.njk" %}

    <p>The answer isn't "LLM everywhere", it's knowing how much freedom to give AI. When to let it loose, and when a deterministic workflow or human being is more appropriate.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="how-i-think-section" aria-labelledby="teams-heading">
    <h2 id="teams-heading">How I work across teams</h2>
    <p>The most valuable design work happens before anything looks like a project. At Dialpad, I inherited a feature factory: siloed PMs, designers executing tickets, and engineers boxed in by technical limitations. We reorganized roadmaps around directional themes instead of disconnected deliverables. We want to ship great products, not our org chart.</p>

    <p>I also partner with teams like support, professional services, and sales. I'd rather hear about a problem early than in an escalation. When a customer asks for something specific, I look for the spirit of the ask and design it for everyone.</p>

    <p>Once work is underway:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>I bring PMs and engineers into the design process early. Brainstorming, diagraming, wireframing, everyone gets input before we go too far in any one direction. The best ideas don't always come from people with design in their title.</li>
      <li>When someone asks for the unreasonable, I lead with "<a href-="https://www.intercom.com/blog/the-orange-juice-test">yes, and here's what it costs</a>." Explain what we'd delay or give up, and help people decide with full information.</li>
      <li>Teaching is part of the job. I've taught non-technical designers GitHub, set up a prototyping sandbox, and helped designers and PMs adopt AI tools within six months.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="how-i-think-section" aria-labelledby="lead-heading">
    <h2 id="lead-heading">How I lead</h2>
    <p>When I first became a manager, I hired good people and stayed out of their way. Some thrived and some stalled, so I got more deliberate: notice what each person needs, coach them on it directly, and adjust as they grow.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Clear, actionable feedback.</strong> I don't sugarcoat critique or avoid hard conversations.</li>
      <li><strong>Designers should think like product people, not production artists.</strong> Craft gets you to senior. Storytelling, business sense, and selling your ideas get you further. I spent two years helping a senior designer make that shift before promoting them to staff.</li>
      <li><strong>Still in the work.</strong> I owned features end to end, even as a people manager. It's hard to coach designers when you've stopped designing.</li>
    </ul>

  </section>

